#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712765 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=- 4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\\\ y(t)&=- 3 t^{2} - 2 \\\\ z(t)&=4 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=5$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=-2\\left(2\\sin(2t)+4t\\cos(2t)-2\\sin(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 6 t \\\\ z'(t)&=2\\left(2\\cos(2t)-4t\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(- 8 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- 6 t\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=100 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{100 t^{2}} \\\\ &=10|t| \\\\ &=10 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{1}^{5}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{5}(10 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[5 t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{5} \\\\ &=\\left(125\\right)-\\left(5\\right) \\\\ &=120\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712773 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\\\ y(t)&=- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ z(t)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3} t^{2}}{2} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=-1$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\le 0$ on this interval, $|t|=-t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=\\left(\\cos t-(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\right) \\\\ &=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=-1\\left(-\\sin t+(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\right) \\\\ &=- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ z'(t)&=\\sqrt{3} t \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(\\sqrt{3} t\\right)^2 \\\\ &=4 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{4 t^{2}} \\\\ &=2|t| \\\\ &=- 2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{-3}^{-1}(- 2 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[- t^{2}\\right]_{-3}^{-1} \\\\ &=\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(-9\\right) \\\\ &=8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712781 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=-\\sqrt{3} t^{2} - 2 \\\\ y(t)&=- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ z(t)&=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=- 2\\sqrt{3} t \\\\ y'(t)&=-2\\left(-\\sin t+(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ z'(t)&=2\\left(\\cos t-(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\right) \\\\ &=2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(- 2\\sqrt{3} t\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=16 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{16 t^{2}} \\\\ &=4|t| \\\\ &=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(4 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[2 t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left(18\\right)-\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712790 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3} t^{2}}{2} + 2 \\\\ y(t)&=- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\\\ z(t)&=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=3$ to $t=4$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=\\sqrt{3} t \\\\ y'(t)&=\\left(\\cos t-(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\right) \\\\ &=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ z'(t)&=\\left(-\\sin t+(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\right) \\\\ &=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(\\sqrt{3} t\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=4 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{4 t^{2}} \\\\ &=2|t| \\\\ &=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{3}^{4}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{3}^{4}(2 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[t^{2}\\right]_{3}^{4} \\\\ &=\\left(16\\right)-\\left(9\\right) \\\\ &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712797 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\\\ y(t)&=\\sqrt{3} t^{2} - 2 \\\\ z(t)&=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=3$ to $t=5$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=-2\\left(-\\sin t+(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=2\\sqrt{3} t \\\\ z'(t)&=2\\left(\\cos t-(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\right) \\\\ &=2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(2\\sqrt{3} t\\right)^2+\\left(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=16 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{16 t^{2}} \\\\ &=4|t| \\\\ &=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{3}^{5}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{3}^{5}(4 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[2 t^{2}\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=\\left(50\\right)-\\left(18\\right) \\\\ &=32\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712806 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red. Diagram is schematic.](images/06/06b1a26e87ee3098d7fee1fda264fb30f67bfe45187b0d2ed94b0e0a79c2877a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}3 e^{t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=2$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both components and simplify the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=3 \\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 3 \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2 \\\\ &=9 e^{2 t}\\left(1\\cos(t)^2+1\\sin(t)^2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad-18 e^{2 t}\\cos(t)\\sin(t) \\\\ &\\quad+9 e^{2 t}\\left(1\\sin(t)^2+1\\cos(t)^2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad+18 e^{2 t}\\cos(t)\\sin(t) \\\\ &=18 e^{2 t}\\left(\\cos(t)^2+\\sin(t)^2\\right) \\\\ &=18 e^{2 t} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=3 \\sqrt{2} e^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe mixed terms cancel. Use the Pythagorean identity, then take the nonnegative square root; the exponential factor is positive.\n\nIntegrate the speed over the parameter interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{1}^{2}3 \\sqrt{2} e^{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[3 \\sqrt{2} e^{t}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} e^{2}\\right)-\\left(3 \\sqrt{2} e\\right) \\\\ &=- 3 \\sqrt{2} e + 3 \\sqrt{2} e^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712815 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red.](images/33/33881da11a22dfce762540152077ec781246851e035a091b8ec9d7f3f03702b0.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}2 - 3 t \\\\ - \\left(t^{2} + 2\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}} - 1\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both components and simplify the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=-3 \\\\ y'(t)&=- 3 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2 \\\\ &=9 t^{4} + 18 t^{2} + 9 \\\\ &=\\left(3 t^{2} + 3\\right)^2 \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=3 t^{2} + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the nonnegative square root. Here $t^2+1>0$.\n\nIntegrate the speed over the parameter interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{1}3 t^{2} + 3\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[t^{3} + 3 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712821 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red. Diagram is schematic.](images/1d/1d9cdd9736c6157b606bcaf05c74dbdb2f29403c8d89bb2220e4c025caff266a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}6 t \\\\ - t^{3} - \\frac{3}{t}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=2$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both components and simplify the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=6 \\\\ y'(t)&=- 3 t^{2} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2 \\\\ &=9 t^{4} + 18 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}} \\\\ &=\\left(3 t^{2} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\right)^2 \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=3 t^{2} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the nonnegative square root.\n\nIntegrate the speed over the parameter interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{1}^{2}3 t^{2} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[t^{3} - \\frac{3}{t}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{13}{2}\\right)-\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712825 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red.](images/07/071854a714bbde918f270519fe93085c7924439841c8c4e225316a59fe827c10.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 \\\\ 2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both components and simplify the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=- 4 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2 \\\\ &=16 t^{2}(\\cos^2(2 t)+\\sin^2(2 t)) \\\\ &=16 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=4|t| \\\\ &=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product-rule terms cancel. Use the Pythagorean identity and $|t|=t$ on the stated interval.\n\nIntegrate the speed over the parameter interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{2}4 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[2 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(8\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712832 :question/problem "![The specified curve segment is blue; its continuation is red.](images/96/96ffae4f794c38cd062149f5f0dea313b605e3a0e3961eaa9f09c16785d7c2a4.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}6 t^{3} - 21 \\\\ - 9 t^{2} - 30\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=- \\sqrt{3}$ to $t=-1$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both components and simplify the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=18 t^{2} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 18 t \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2 \\\\ &=324 t^{2}(t^2+1) \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=18|t|\\sqrt{t^2+1} \\\\ &=- 18 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSpeed is nonnegative; the factor $|t|$ comes from $\\sqrt{t^2}$. Here $|t|=-t$ on the entire interval.\n\nUse $u=t^2+1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{- \\sqrt{3}}^{-1}- 18 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=-9\\int_{4}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[- 6 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right]_{4}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(- 12 \\sqrt{2}\\right)-\\left(-48\\right) \\\\ &=48 - 12 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712914 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 2 y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 2 x y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 2 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 2 x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 2 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=-2 \\\\ h(z)&=- 2 z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x y - 2 z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712919 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(x + z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(y - 1\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=x + z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=y - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=x y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x + z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=x + z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=y z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x y + y z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=-1 \\\\ h(z)&=- z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x y + y z - z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712936 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(2 x + 2 z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(2 y + 2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=2 y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2 x + 2 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=2 y + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=2 x y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2 x + 2 z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=2 x + 2 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=2 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(2 z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=2 y z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=2 y z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x y + 2 y z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=2 y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=2 \\\\ h(z)&=2 z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x y + 2 y z + 2 z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712938 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- x - z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(1 - y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- x - z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=1 - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- x y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- x - z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- x - z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=- y z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=- y z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- x y - y z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=- y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=1 \\\\ h(z)&=z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- x y - y z + z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712942 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 3 x - 2 z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(1 - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 3 y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x - 2 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=1 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 3 x y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x - 2 z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x - 2 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 2 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 2 z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=- 2 y z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=- 2 y z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 x y - 2 y z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=- 2 y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=1 \\\\ h(z)&=z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 x y - 2 y z + z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712980 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 3 y - 3\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 3 y - 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 3 y - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 3 x y - 3 x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 x y - 3 x+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=-2 \\\\ h(z)&=- 2 z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 x y - 3 x - 2 z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712981 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 2 y z^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 2 x z^{2} - 6 z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 4 x y z - 6 y - 12\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 2 y z^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 2 x z^{2} - 6 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=- 4 x y z - 6 y - 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 2 y z^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 2 x y z^{2}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 2 x z^{2}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 2 x z^{2} - 6 z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 2 x z^{2}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 2 x z^{2} - 6 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 6 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 6 z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=- 6 y z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=- 6 y z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x y z^{2} - 6 y z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=- 4 x y z - 6 y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=-12 \\\\ h(z)&=- 12 z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x y z^{2} - 6 y z - 12 z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712990 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(4 x y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(2 x^{2} - 8 y z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 4 y^{2} - 4\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=4 x y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2 x^{2} - 8 y z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=- 4 y^{2} - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(4 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=2 x^{2} y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2 x^{2}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2 x^{2} - 8 y z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 x^{2}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=2 x^{2} - 8 y z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 8 y z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 8 y z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=- 4 y^{2} z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=- 4 y^{2} z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x^{2} y - 4 y^{2} z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=- 4 y^{2}+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=-4 \\\\ h(z)&=- 4 z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x^{2} y - 4 y^{2} z - 4 z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712995 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(2 y z^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(2 x z^{2} - z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(4 x y z - y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=2 y z^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2 x z^{2} - z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=4 x y z - y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(2 y z^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=2 x y z^{2}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2 x z^{2}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=2 x z^{2} - z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 x z^{2}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=2 x z^{2} - z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=- y z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=- y z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x y z^{2} - y z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=4 x y z - y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=0 \\\\ h(z)&=C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x y z^{2} - y z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713036 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y)\\,i+(- x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it.\n\n*Hint: $r^{\\prime}(t)=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$. Substitute both coordinates into the field, then take the dot product.*" :question/worked-solution "Use the vector line-integral formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates $x=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ into both field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))&=((\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(- (\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j\\\\&=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each path component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\y^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply matching components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&\\quad+(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the products using $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ where applicable:\n\n$$\nF(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)=1.\n$$\n\nKeep the supplied parameter limits. The required integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{2}\\bigl[(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&\\quad+(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\bigr]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713039 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(2 x y^{2})\\,i+(4 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+(e^{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,3]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it.\n\n*Hint: $r^{\\prime}(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,i+(e^{t})\\,j$. Substitute both coordinates into the field, then take the dot product.*" :question/worked-solution "Use the vector line-integral formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=e^{t}$ into both field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))&=(2 (\\sqrt{t}) (e^{t})^{2})\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(4 (e^{t}))\\,j\\\\&=(2 \\sqrt{t} e^{2 t})\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(4 e^{t})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each path component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}},\\\\y^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t})=e^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply matching components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=(2 \\sqrt{t} e^{2 t})\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\\\&\\quad+(4 e^{t})(e^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the products:\n\n$$\nF(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)=5 e^{2 t}.\n$$\n\nKeep the supplied parameter limits. The required integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{1}^{3}\\bigl[(2 \\sqrt{t} e^{2 t})\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\\\&\\quad+(4 e^{t})(e^{t})\\bigr]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713049 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(2 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it.\n\n*Hint: $r^{\\prime}(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$. Substitute both coordinates into the field, then take the dot product.*" :question/worked-solution "Use the vector line-integral formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates $x=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ into both field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))&=(2 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(2 (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j\\\\&=(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each path component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\y^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})=- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply matching components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&\\quad+(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the products using $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ where applicable:\n\n$$\nF(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nKeep the supplied parameter limits. The required integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{1}\\bigl[(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&\\quad+(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\bigr]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713053 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(2 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it.\n\n*Hint: $r^{\\prime}(t)=(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j$. Substitute both coordinates into the field, then take the dot product.*" :question/worked-solution "Use the vector line-integral formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates $x=t$ and $y=t^{2}$ into both field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))&=((t^{2})^{2})\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(2 (t)^{2})\\,j\\\\&=(t^{4})\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(2 t^{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each path component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)=1,\\\\y^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})=2 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply matching components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=(t^{4})(1)\\\\&\\quad+(2 t^{2})(2 t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the products:\n\n$$\nF(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)=t^{4} + 4 t^{3}.\n$$\n\nKeep the supplied parameter limits. The required integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{2}\\bigl[(t^{4})(1)\\\\&\\quad+(2 t^{2})(2 t)\\bigr]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713055 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\,i+(- 2 y - 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it.\n\n*Hint: $r^{\\prime}(t)=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$. Substitute both coordinates into the field, then take the dot product.*" :question/worked-solution "Use the vector line-integral formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates $x=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ into both field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))&=(- 2 (\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(- 2 (\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) - 1)\\,j\\\\&=(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i\\\\&\\quad+(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each path component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\y^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply matching components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&\\quad+(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1)(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the products using $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ where applicable:\n\n$$\nF(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nKeep the supplied parameter limits. The required integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{1}\\bigl[(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&\\quad+(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1)(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\bigr]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713109 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(- 2 y - 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(6 - 2 x\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 2 y - 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=- 2 x y - 2 x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=- 2 x+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(6 - 2 x\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(- 2 x\\bigr)\\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(6\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=6 y+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 2 x y - 2 x + 6 y+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 2 y - 2,\\\\\nf_y&=6 - 2 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713110 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(x\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(y\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=x y+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=x+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(x\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(x\\bigr)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(0\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=0+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x y+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=y,\\\\\nf_y&=x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713124 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(y + 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(x\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(y + 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=x y + x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=x+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(x\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(x\\bigr)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(0\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=0+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x y + x+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=y + 1,\\\\\nf_y&=x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713131 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(- 2 y - 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(- 2 x - 6\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 2 y - 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=- 2 x y - 2 x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=- 2 x+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(- 2 x - 6\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(- 2 x\\bigr)\\\\\n&=-6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(-6\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=- 6 y+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 2 x y - 2 x - 6 y+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 2 y - 2,\\\\\nf_y&=- 2 x - 6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713137 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(y - 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(x + 3\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(y - 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=x y - x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=x+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(x + 3\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(x\\bigr)\\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(3\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=3 y+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x y - x + 3 y+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=y - 1,\\\\\nf_y&=x + 3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713142 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(2 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(x^{2} - 4 x y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(2 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=x^{2} y - 2 x^{2} - 2 x y^{2} - \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)}+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=x^{2} - 4 x y - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(x^{2} - 4 x y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(x^{2} - 4 x y - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=x^{2} y - 2 x^{2} - 2 x y^{2}\\\\&- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=2 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)},\\\\\nf_y&=x^{2} - 4 x y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713149 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(- 2 x y - y^{2} - y e^{x y}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(- x^{2} - 2 x y - x e^{x y}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 2 x y - y^{2} - y e^{x y}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=- x^{2} y - x y^{2} - e^{x y}+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=- x^{2} - 2 x y - x e^{x y}+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(- x^{2} - 2 x y - x e^{x y}\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(- x^{2} - 2 x y - x e^{x y}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(0\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=0+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=- x^{2} y - x y^{2}\\\\&- e^{x y}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 2 x y - y^{2} - y e^{x y},\\\\\nf_y&=- x^{2} - 2 x y - x e^{x y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713151 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(- 4 x y - 4 x + 2 y^{2} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} + 4 x y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 4 x y - 4 x + 2 y^{2} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=- 2 x^{2} y - 2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2} - 2 \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)}+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=- 2 x^{2} + 4 x y - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} + 4 x y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} + 4 x y - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=- 2 x^{2} y - 2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2}\\\\&- 2 \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 4 x y - 4 x + 2 y^{2} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)},\\\\\nf_y&=- 2 x^{2} + 4 x y - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713160 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(- 4 x y - 4 x + y^{2} - y e^{x y}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y - x e^{x y} - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 4 x y - 4 x + y^{2} - y e^{x y}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=- 2 x^{2} y - 2 x^{2} + x y^{2} - e^{x y}+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y - x e^{x y}+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y - x e^{x y} - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y - x e^{x y}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(- \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=- 2 x^{2} y - 2 x^{2} + x y^{2}\\\\&- e^{x y} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 4 x y - 4 x + y^{2} - y e^{x y},\\\\\nf_y&=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x y - x e^{x y} - \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713168 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(- 2 x y + 2 y^{2} - 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(- x^{2} + 4 x y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 2 x y + 2 y^{2} - 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=- x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2} - x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=- x^{2} + 4 x y+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(- x^{2} + 4 x y\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(- x^{2} + 4 x y\\bigr)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(0\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=0+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2} - x+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 2 x y + 2 y^{2} - 1,\\\\\nf_y&=- x^{2} + 4 x y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713172 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2} + x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=x^{2} + 4 x y+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(x^{2} + 4 x y\\bigr)\\\\\n&=3 y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(3 y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=y^{3}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2} + x + y^{3}+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 1,\\\\\nf_y&=x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713177 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(x^{2} + 4 x y - 3 y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2} + 2 x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=x^{2} + 4 x y+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(x^{2} + 4 x y - 3 y^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(x^{2} + 4 x y\\bigr)\\\\\n&=- 3 y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 3 y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=- y^{3}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2} + 2 x - y^{3}+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 2,\\\\\nf_y&=x^{2} + 4 x y - 3 y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713184 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(- 4 x y - y^{2} - 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y + 6 y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The vector components are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrate the first component with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant. Retain an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(- 4 x y - y^{2} - 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=- 2 x^{2} y - x y^{2} - 2 x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y+g'(y)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y + 6 y^{2}\\bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y\\bigr)\\\\\n&=6 y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate the remaining function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y)&=\\int\\bigl(6 y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dy\\\\\n&=2 y^{3}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} y - x y^{2} - 2 x + 2 y^{3}+C\n$$\n\nBoth components check:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 4 x y - y^{2} - 2,\\\\\nf_y&=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y + 6 y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715104 :question/problem "Given $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} - 3 x + 2 \\left(y + 3\\right)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$." :question/worked-solution "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=4 x - 3 \\\\ f_{y}&=4 y + 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 x - 3&=0 \\\\ 4 y + 12&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x,\\ v=y + 3$. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 u&=3 \\\\ u&=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ v&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Recover the original coordinates using $x=u,\\ y=v - 3$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(\\frac{3}{4},-3)$. Each satisfies every component equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715106 :question/problem "Given $f(x,y)=2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\ln{\\left(\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\right)}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$." :question/worked-solution "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=\\frac{2 \\left(x + 2\\right)}{\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} + 2} \\\\ f_{y}&=4 y + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\left(x + 2\\right)}{\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} + 2}&=0 \\\\ 4 y + 4&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x + 2,\\ v=y + 1$. Since $u^2+2>0$, the first component vanishes exactly when $u=0$. The other equation gives $v=0$. Recover the original coordinates using $x=u - 2,\\ y=v - 1$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(-2,-1)$. Each satisfies every component equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715133 :question/problem "Given $f(x,y)=\\left(\\left(x - 3\\right)^{2} - \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}\\right) e^{x - 3}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$." :question/worked-solution "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=\\left(2 x + \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2} - \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right) e^{x - 3} \\\\ f_{y}&=- 2 \\left(y - 1\\right) e^{x - 3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\left(2 x + \\left(x - 3\\right)^{2} - \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right) e^{x - 3}&=0 \\\\ - 2 \\left(y - 1\\right) e^{x - 3}&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x - 3,\\ v=y - 1$. Since $e^{u}>0$, divide both equations by this factor. The second equation gives $v=0$. Substitution into the first gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u \\left(u + 2\\right)&=0 \\\\ u&=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad u=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep both factors; dividing by $u$ would lose a point. Recover the original coordinates using $x=u + 3,\\ y=v + 1$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(3,1)$ and $(1,1)$. Each satisfies every component equation."}]}
